/// United States 529 college savings plans.
///
/// A 529 plan is a tax-deferred account funded with after-tax money. What was
/// put in is its basis (`basis cost`, whether it was your own money or a gift
/// from a grandparent), and the growth is untaxed while it stays inside, and
/// untaxed when it comes out to pay a qualified education expense. Anything
/// else that comes out has its earnings taxed and a 10% tax added.
///
/// A withdrawal relieves contributions and earnings pro rata, so its earnings
/// are the plan's earnings in the same proportion (Form 1099-Q). Spend from a
/// plan straight into a place of kind `education`, and the withdrawal is
/// qualified. Growth reaches the plan through a place of kind `market`, and is
/// never counted as a contribution.
///
/// The figures are community-maintained data. Verify them against the IRS
/// (irs.gov, Rev. Proc. for the annual gift-tax exclusion) before relying on
/// them, and correct them in a pull request when they are wrong.
system us/529

use us
use std


/// A 529 qualified tuition program (IRC §529).
kind 529-plan : tax-deferred
  basis cost
  select prorata
  liquidity 5d
  pays education
  has beneficiary entity

  /// Contributions above the annual gift-tax exclusion count as a gift to the
  /// beneficiary, which may need a gift-tax return (Form 709, IRC §2503(b)).
  /// This deposit took the year's contributions over the exclusion. What
  /// counts is money paid in, yours or a relative's, and not the market's
  /// growth or a transfer from another plan.
  ///
  /// To fix: a gift of up to five years of the exclusion may be made at once by
  /// electing to spread it (IRC §529(c)(2)(B)); if you have, mark the deposit
  /// `!`.
  law gift-exclusion
    on in
    when not from is market | tax-deferred
    count amount as 529-contributions
    warn value(tally(529-contributions), USD) <= gift-exclusion[year] "529 contributions over the annual gift exclusion"

  /// A withdrawal that did not pay a qualified education expense owes an
  /// additional 10% of its earnings (IRC §529(c)(6), §530(d)(4)). This
  /// withdrawal went somewhere other than a place of kind `education`, so it is
  /// priced at that 10%, and its earnings count as income.
  ///
  /// To fix: to reimburse yourself for something the plan should have paid, or
  /// when an exception applies (a scholarship, a death or disability of the
  /// beneficiary), keep the withdrawal and mark it `!` with the reason.
  law nonqualified-529-penalty
    on gain
    when gain > empty
    when not to is market
    require purpose is #education or to is 529-plan else owe 10% * gain to irs by date(year + 1, 4, 15) as nonqualified-529-penalty "529 withdrawal that did not pay an education expense"


// The annual gift-tax exclusion per recipient (IRC §2503(b)).
// Rev. Proc. 2023-34 (2024), Rev. Proc. 2024-40 (2025), Rev. Proc. 2025-32 (2026).
// verify: the 2026 figure against Rev. Proc. 2025-32.
param gift-exclusion
  2024 18_000 USD
  2025 19_000 USD
  2026 19_000 USD
